Make the FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN default actually reach an existing installation.

Proposed follow-up to tvheadend#2117, "bullet 1" of the CodeRabbit remark on
src/transcoding/codec/profile_class.c:121.  Ours, on top of patch 100.

WHAT THE PR DOES NOW

  profile.c:170        profile->profile = FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN;  (before idnode_load)
  profile_class.c:121  if (self->profile != FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN && ...)

The default is assigned before idnode_load(), and the relaxed test lets an
explicit profile 0 reach the encoder again -- which is what the remark asked
for, 0 being Baseline for nvenc and v4l2m2m H.264.

WHY IT DOES NOT TAKE EFFECT ON AN EXISTING INSTALLATION

idnode_load() runs after that assignment and overwrites it with whatever the
saved config holds -- and saved configs do hold the field.  On a DSM install
carrying the shipped profile set, all 13 files under
/var/packages/tvheadend/var/codec/ contain

    "profile": 0

webtv-aac included.  The -99 default therefore applies only to a profile
created from scratch, never to one loaded from disk, which is every profile on
every existing installation.  (tvh_codec_profile_load() does go through
tvh_codec_profile_create(), so the comment claiming the default reaches
brand-new nodes only is no longer accurate about the mechanism, even though its
conclusion holds for the reason above.)

Where those zeros come from matters, because a fresh install does not show the
problem: data/conf/transcoder/codecs, the shipped profile tree, carries no
"profile" field at all, so a profile created from it keeps the -99 default and
is saved with it.  The stored zeros are the work of earlier versions, where the
field defaulted to 0 and idnode_save() wrote it out.  Testing on a fresh config
therefore confirms the new default while leaving the upgrade path untested --
which is the path every existing user is on.

The consequences are not symmetrical:

  aac    aac.c:26 lists FF_AV_PROFILE_AAC_MAIN, which is 0, and tvh_codec_init()
         keeps a codec's own list, so _is_profile_available(aac, 0) is true and
         "profile=0" is emitted.  ffmpeg 8.1.2's native aac encoder answers

           [aac] Profile not supported!
           [enc:aac] Error while opening encoder - maybe incorrect parameters
           Error sending frames to consumers: Invalid argument

         and the audio stream never opens.  This is precisely the failure the
         "profile > 0" test was masking, restored for every upgraded install.

  nvenc  nvenc.c:62 defines NV_ENC_H264_PROFILE_BASELINE as 0 and
         nvenc_h264_profiles[] lists it, as v4l2-m2m.c:23 does for
         V4L2_H264_PROFILE_BASELINE, so for those two encoders 0 passes
         _is_profile_available() legitimately: an existing profile now has
         Baseline imposed where the encoder used to choose.  That is the mirror
         image of the bug the remark reported, and no gating of another codec's
         list can reach it.

  rest   for codecs whose list holds no 0 (libx264 and friends) nothing is
         emitted; _is_profile_available() merely logs a warning per open.

WHERE THE "profile > 0" TEST CAME FROM

The test the review flagged was added for this same aac failure, in June 2026.
webtv-aac carried no "profile" field, so it loaded as 0; AAC_MAIN being 0 and
listed as available, "profile=0" reached the encoder, which refused it -- audio
dead on every webtv-*-aac-* profile, software paths included.  The reasoning
recorded with that fix was that AAC_MAIN is the only profile value equal to 0
across the tables, h264 starting at 66 and hevc at 1, so a "profile > 0" test
could not regress anything.  That holds for the generic tables; it missed the
per-encoder ones, where nvenc_h264_profiles and v4l2m2m_h264_profiles both
declare Baseline as 0.  The review is right about that, and the new default is
the right shape of fix.

A config-level fix was weighed at the time and set aside as fragile, and
because it would not repair the instance already saved on disk.  That second
objection turns out to apply to the new default as well, and the migration
below is what answers it: once, persistently, for the configs already written.

WHAT THIS PATCH PROPOSES

Three separable changes.  The first is the one that makes bullet 1 effective.

  src/config.c
    config_migrate_v26 rewrites a stored "profile": 0 to -99 across codec/*.
    A one-shot migration rather than a test in the load path, so that a user
    who does select profile 0 afterwards is obeyed; a test would quietly undo
    that choice on every restart.  The rewrite is lossless by construction:
    under the previous "profile > 0" code a stored 0 was never emitted, so -99
    is exactly the behaviour those configs already had.  Migrations run from
    config_init() (main.c:1269) ahead of codec_init() (1305), so the codec
    subsystem reads the rewritten files.

  src/transcoding/codec/profile_class.c
    Drop the commented-out test and correct the stale comment above.

  src/transcoding/codec/codecs/aac.c
    Gate Main and LTP on LIBAVCODEC_VERSION_MAJOR < 62.  uknunknown's own fix,
    taken as he wrote it: ffmpeg 8 removed both coders, so naming that as the
    cause is better than dropping them outright, and it keeps them on ffmpeg 7
    where they still encode.  The package's ffmpeg 8.1.3 reports major 62, so
    the gate excludes exactly the two that went away; measured against it, of
    the four profiles offered only LC and MPEG2_LC open, while Main (0) and LTP
    (3) both answer "Profile not supported!".  LTP was already broken
    beforehand; Main becomes broken by the new default, having been silently
    ignored until then.

    With the list gated, _is_profile_available() settles the aac case on its
    own, 0 no longer being in it -- which is why the migration below is not
    about aac.  It is about the encoders where 0 is a real profile.

WHAT STILL WANTS HARDWARE

The migration itself needs no GPU: restart with the profiles above and check
that codec/* holds -99 and that aac streams open.  What hardware adds is
confirmation that an explicitly chosen profile now reaches the QSV and VAAPI
encoders and is accepted -- the half of bullet 1 no config change can show.

--- src/config.c.orig
+++ src/config.c
@@ -1514,6 +1514,38 @@ config_migrate_v25 ( void )
   }
 }
 
+/*
+ * v25 -> v26 : codec profile "profile": 0 used to mean "unset"
+ *
+ * Every codec profile config written before the AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN default
+ * carries "profile": 0 -- not a choice, just the zeroed field.  The encoder
+ * options were built with a "profile > 0" test, so a stored 0 behaved exactly
+ * as "no profile": the encoder picked its own.  Now that an explicit 0 is
+ * honoured (it is a real profile for some encoders), those stored zeros would
+ * start reaching the encoder and change, or break, existing streams.  Rewrite
+ * them to AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN, which is the behaviour they have always had; a
+ * user who does want profile 0 can select it again and now be obeyed.
+ *
+ * -99 is spelled out because this file builds without libavcodec.
+ */
+static void
+config_migrate_v26 ( void )
+{
+  htsmsg_t *c, *e;
+  htsmsg_field_t *f;
+
+  /* Codec profiles are loaded after the migrations, so rewrite them on disk. */
+  if ((c = hts_settings_load("codec")) == NULL)
+    return;
+  HTSMSG_FOREACH(f, c) {
+    if (!(e = htsmsg_field_get_map(f))) continue;
+    if (htsmsg_get_s32_or_default(e, "profile", -99) != 0) continue;
+    htsmsg_set_s32(e, "profile", -99);
+    hts_settings_save(e, "codec/%s", htsmsg_field_name(f));
+  }
+  htsmsg_destroy(c);
+}
+
 /*
  * Perform backup
  */
@@ -1640,7 +1672,8 @@ static const config_migrate_t config_migrate_table[] = {
   config_migrate_v22,
   config_migrate_v23,
   config_migrate_v24,
-  config_migrate_v25
+  config_migrate_v25,
+  config_migrate_v26
 };
 
 /*
--- src/transcoding/codec/profile_class.c.orig
+++ src/transcoding/codec/profile_class.c
@@ -111,14 +111,11 @@ static int
 tvh_codec_profile_base_open(TVHCodecProfile *self, AVDictionary **opts)
 {
     AV_DICT_SET_TVH_REQUIRE_META(LST_NONE, opts, 1);
-    // profile is set and is available.
-    // Profile 0 is AAC Main, but it is also the zeroed value of a profile
-    // loaded from a config without an explicit "profile" field (the class
-    // default FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN is only applied to brand-new nodes). The
-    // native aac encoder rejects Main ("Profile not supported!" -> EINVAL),
-    // so only emit a positive (explicitly chosen) profile and let the encoder
-    // pick its own default otherwise.
-    //if (self->profile > 0 &&
+    // Emit the profile only when one was actually chosen.
+    // FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN is the default both for a new node and for a
+    // config carrying no "profile" field, and config_migrate_v26 rewrites the
+    // zeros older versions stored. 0 itself is a real profile for some
+    // encoders (nvenc and v4l2m2m H.264 Baseline), so it must pass through.
     if (self->profile != FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN &&
         _is_profile_available(self->codec, self->profile)) {
         AV_DICT_SET_INT(LST_CODEC, opts, "profile", self->profile, 0);
--- src/transcoding/codec/codecs/aac.c.orig
+++ src/transcoding/codec/codecs/aac.c
@@ -23,10 +23,18 @@
 
 /* aac ====================================================================== */
 // see aacenc_profiles[] ffmpeg-7.0/libavcodec/aacenctab.h + AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN
